WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:05.800
Good morning Hank, It's Friday December 9th 2016, the beginning of the 10th annual Project for Awesome

00:00:05.800 --> 00:00:08.620
You can head over to projectforawesome.com now to watch the 48 hour live stream

00:00:08.720 --> 00:00:11.040
and to watch and vote for P4A Videos

00:00:11.040 --> 00:00:16.600
or head over to projectforawesome.com/donate to donate and get great perks from socks to signed books

00:00:16.600 --> 00:00:20.900
So during the first half of the Project for Awesome we're raising money for Save the Children and Partners in Health

00:00:20.900 --> 00:00:25.860
Two of the most highlyregarded and efficient non profits working in global health and education

00:00:25.860 --> 00:00:29.640
I wanna give you an idea of what they do and given that this is the 10th Project for Awesome

00:00:29.640 --> 00:00:32.300
I thought we could we could look at the difference 10 years can make.

00:00:32.300 --> 00:00:38.080
Like, 10 years ago the Rwinkwavu Hospital in in Rwanda was extremely dilapidated and barely functioning.

00:00:38.080 --> 00:00:41.900
Today it's a safe and sanitary hospital providing high quality care.

00:00:41.900 --> 00:00:45.600
Ten years ago in Rwanda's Burera district there were no hospitals at all.

00:00:45.600 --> 00:00:50.660
Today they don't just have a hospital, they have a hospital with a cancer center and a neonatal intensive care unit

00:00:50.660 --> 00:00:54.840
and Partners in Health lead the drive to build and fund and staff both those hospitals

00:00:54.880 --> 00:01:01.880
They also work around the world to train and employ community health workers like Muhammad from Sierra Leone and tens of thousands of people like him

00:01:07.080 --> 00:01:10.360
and it's pretty astonishing just how effective this work has been.

00:01:10.420 --> 00:01:17.220
A decade ago almost %13 of children in Sierra Leone died before the age of 5, today it's under %9.

00:01:17.280 --> 00:01:21.640
In Rwanda life expectancy was 56 a decade ago today it's 64.

00:01:21.640 --> 00:01:27.280
and there are similar stories around the world. In the last 10 years the global absolute poverty rate has fallen by more than half.

00:01:27.280 --> 00:01:32.860
In 2006 %18 of the world's children were under weight because of malnutrition, today it's down to %14

00:01:32.860 --> 00:01:38.600
and world wide 4 million fewer children died last year than died in 2006.

00:01:38.600 --> 00:01:39.980
but there's a coldness to numbers. Right like

00:01:39.980 --> 00:01:45.680
it's hard to imagine the millions of parents who won't have to suffer the unbearable grief of losing a child

00:01:45.680 --> 00:01:50.720
or the tens of thousands of kids who wouldn't have mothers if not for the gains in maternal health in the last decade

00:01:50.720 --> 00:01:57.020
When you hear for instance that Save the Children provides nutrition and health care and educational opportunities to millions of kids every year

00:01:57.020 --> 00:01:58.200
That's not a picture

00:01:58.200 --> 00:02:01.420
I think sometimes statistics can be a barrier to empathy

00:02:01.420 --> 00:02:02.620
rather than a path to .

00:02:02.680 --> 00:02:04.360
So let's look at one family.

00:02:04.360 --> 00:02:08.040
Save the Children is very active in the ongoing South Sudanese refugee crisis

00:02:08.040 --> 00:02:10.760
Providing education and safe spaces for children

00:02:10.760 --> 00:02:14.400
and also trying to reunite families who have been torn apart by war

00:02:14.400 --> 00:02:18.760
This women, Grace, was separated from her two daughters during an attack in her community

00:02:18.760 --> 00:02:20.540
and she hadn't seen her kids in 2 years

00:02:20.540 --> 00:02:22.540
in fact she didn't even know if they'd survived

00:02:22.540 --> 00:02:25.820
Save the Children case workers were able to connect Grace to her two daughters

00:02:25.820 --> 00:02:28.960
Through a database of missing kids and reunite the family

00:02:28.960 --> 00:02:32.300
This moment didn't end the war or end the refugee crisis

00:02:32.300 --> 00:02:35.020
but for this one family it meant everything.

00:02:35.020 --> 00:02:38.700
Whether reuniting families or saving lives through health care and nutrition

00:02:38.700 --> 00:02:42.180
these little successes are not little when they're yours

00:02:42.180 --> 00:02:46.280
of course the scope of the challenge before us remains overwhelming as Muhammed put it

00:02:49.280 --> 00:02:52.340
but our support for Save the Children and Partners in Health enables them to hire

00:02:52.340 --> 00:02:55.500
more community health workers, build more hospitals and schools

00:02:55.500 --> 00:02:56.960
and reunite more families.

00:02:56.960 --> 00:02:59.720
And maybe a decade from now we'll be able to look back

00:02:59.720 --> 00:03:03.260
and see how those little successes were part of a much larger one

00:03:03.260 --> 00:03:04.780
for the first half of the Project for Awesome

00:03:05.420 --> 00:03:09.180
every dollar donated up to $300,000 will be matched to $4

00:03:09.180 --> 00:03:13.680
you can have have a huge impact right now at projectforawesome.com/donate

00:03:13.680 --> 00:03:18.180
and also you can get great perks there's everything from tote bags to commemorative pressed pennies

00:03:18.180 --> 00:03:21.040
to the first new writing I've released in several years

00:03:21.040 --> 00:03:25.340
during the second half of the Project for Awesome we"ll be raising money for charities chosen by you

00:03:25.340 --> 00:03:27.960
via your votes at projectforawesome.com

00:03:27.960 --> 00:03:29.840
so head over there to watch and vote, thank you

